Dear list,

I thought it appropriate I use this most public space to acknowledge the
EXCELLENT technical support I have received from Mathsoft further to my
posted query/complaint about the documentation clashing with the
implementation., in 5.0 for Unix.

If anyone wants details of the answer to my question (how to make
.S.chapters actually refer to chapters) I will pass on a copy of the
patch to the global .S.init supplied to me by Mathsoft. Or, just wait
until 5.1.

As someone politely pointed out, one can instead read the V&R online
complements where on page 2 it is clearly stated that the initialisation
process does not follow Chambers. Well and good, had I read this first I
would have been saved some frustration. However, the mere observation
that it doesn't work is not a fix. By this I mean no disrespect
whatsoever to V&R; why should they fix it? We don't pay them for all the
work they do for us.

I remain somewhat concerned that someone should point me to the
Complements as though to a FAQ for something in the public domain. Splus
is a COMMERCIAL product. I expect the documentation to reflect this
status; that is what we are paying for. Surely at least the knowledge
within the Complements should be shipped with the product when it is
known. An interesting copyright question if the documentation officially
referred to the V&R website (or even Statlib or this mailing list).

I am frankly stunned at how docilely we accept second best when it comes
to software. I received only three emails reflecting support for my
initial indignation. I wonder if we had all paid for this product out of
our own pockets, what our collective reaction would have been. Or is our
acceptance of software mediocrity deeper than that? Have we been
windozed into submission?

I am still of the opinion that we are beta (gamma?) testing 5.0. I'm
pleased with Mathsoft's instant fix to my query, I'm pleased it will be
fixed in 5.1, but I'm even more pleased we can still run 3.4 in the
meantime. Any time I have to spend on product development I would rather
spend on something in the public domain. 

Simon Fear
