From: dhk at paradox.psych.columbia.edu (Dave Krantz)
Date: Tue, 02 Mar 1999 12:13:08 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] Most efficient (best) way to...?
In-Reply-To: <4.1.19990302080703.0095c410@odin.cc.pdx.edu>
Message-ID: <199903021713.MAA19511@paradox.psych.columbia.edu>

I seldom have occasion to construct a variable of the sort that
you want, but when I do, I use the tapply function:

   group <- tapply(rep(1, nrow(mydata)), list(sex, species))

Ordinarily, tapply() takes a third argument, the function that
you wish to apply to each stratum defined by the list of indices,
but when that argument is omitted, the numerical index you want
is returned.  The first argument serves no function in this call
and you can use any vector of the proper length, e.g., (1:length(sex))
would also do.  (It would make sense to just write a function that
does this rather than taking advantage of an obscure feature of
tapply.)

Dave Krantz
