> From: Gianluca Marchi <gianluca at lunet.it>
> I write the following commands:
> >sub.set<-"!is.na(x1) & !is.na(x2) & !is.na(x3) & x3>15"
> >sbset<-eval(parse(text=sub.set))
> >sbset
>  [1] 0
> 

Remember that quick shell escape with the ! at the beginning of the line?
That is being recognized by the evaluator when you write your expression
with the ! at the beginning.  

For example, try (in Unix):

        eval(parse(text="!ls"))

and you will get a file listing for your working directory, while

        eval(parse(text="(!ls)"))

tries to evaluate the expression !ls (which of course gives an error because
ls is a function).
