From Jean_Adams at usgs.gov  Wed Mar  3 20:59:14 1999
From: Jean_Adams at usgs.gov (Jean Adams)
Date: Wed, 03 Mar 1999 13:59:14 -0700
Subject: [S] Summary: Recommendations for PC
Message-ID: <00015E4D.@usgs.gov>

     
     Thanks to all those who responded to my query: Steven Dubnoff, John 
     Fennick, Andy White, Bob Dorazio, Harald Fekjær, Brian D. Ripley, and 
     G. Stephen Few.  The responses and my original post are below.
     
     Just about everyone recommended Windows NT, so that's the route I 
     took.  The other recommendations led me to take the approach of "get 
     whatever I can afford" in terms of CPU and RAM.  I did just that and 
     got dual Pentium II 450 MHz processors, with 256 MB RAM, a 16 MB video 
     card, and two SCSI hard drives, 9.1 and 4.5 GB (I'll use one for 
     backup).  A local computer place put it together for me (in my case, 
     that was cheaper than getting one from Gateway, Dell, or Compaq).  I 
     just got it set up yesterday, so I don't have a lot of experience with 
     it yet.  But, I did do one quick little comparison in S-PLUS between 
     my old and new computer:
          y <- rnorm(1000)
          x <- rnorm(1000)
          out <- numeric(1000)
          date()
          for(i in 1:1000) {
              yb <- sample(y, 1000, T)
              xb <- sample(x, 1000, T)
              out[i] <- lm(yb ~ xb)$pvalue
              }
          date()
     My old PC (167 MHz, 128 MB RAM, Windows 95) carried out the for() loop 
     in 154 seconds, my new PC did the same in 42 seconds.
     
     JVA
     
     ----------
     Responses:
     ----------
     
     Steven Dubnoff <sdubnoff at circlesys.com>
     
     I highly recommend the following site:
             http://www.tomshardware.com
     for very astute and unbiased hardware recommendations.  In particular, 
     note what he says about the price/performance of the new Celeron 400, 
     which may just be this month's hardware bargain.
     
     ---
     
     John Fennick <jhfslf at swva.net>
     
     A few comments per your request:
     I do not have WIN 95/98 experience but know from friends that WIN NT 
     4.0 (what I use) is more stable in general than those. Still there are 
     crashes but less than even with my old Win 3.11. Although the system 
     is more touchy under frequent new application additions/deletions and 
     I do a fair amount of that, always playing with new releases. Gurus 
     claim that the various "undelete" scripts really don't, and some OS 
     files get messed up as a result. Fair warning, when they get BAD, a 
     complete disc re-format and install is the only sure cure - PAIN PAIN 
     PAIN. I have to face that soon, next week?, after about one year with 
     the current arrangement.
     
     I use NT 4.0, SP 3 (which IS a must, and SP 4 has just been made 
     available but no experience here yet). I have 256 M RAM, and two 
     Pentium II, 266 MHz processors. Having two is of no benefit in S 
     directly, but you mentioned multi-processing and I do realize speed 
     benefits from them in such cases. I also use a very large paging 
     buffer (1 GB) but can't really comment on the worth of that. I have 
     not recently been into large data sets. But disc space is cheap these 
     days.
     
     Here say: The chip set used in conjunction with the Pentium family is 
     supposed to be critical for best performance. I'm on very weak ground 
     here, but you might explore that.
     
     As for video RAM, I get by nicely with 4 M, But for big graphics, my 
     experience is that simple RAM is more crucial. I experienced 
     noticeable improvements over the years as I went from 16 to 32 to 64 
     to 256. Currently, two otherwise identical machines in use here, one 
     with 96 and one with 256 appear comparable on that score.
     
     ---
     
     Andy White <andy_white at hmsa.com>
     
     SCSI drive
     largest BUS speed you can get
     beaucoup RAM 128 mb plus
     450 MHz
     
     I suggest NT because it's VERY stable and IF 1 program crashes another 
     does not .. (tho troublesome having to login as admin vs user)
     
     ---
     
     Bob Dorazio <Bob_Dorazio at usgs.gov>
     
     Attached are the specs for a workstation I purchased about a year ago. 
      Then it was true that
     
     "Several manufacturers, including Compaq, Dell, Digital, Hewlett 
     Packard, IBM, and Intergraph, produce Windows NT/X86 technical 
     workstations that contain industry-standard components, such as PCI 
     controllers and Intel Pentium II processors.  However, Compaq's 
     Professional Workstation 6000 is the only one that meets the 
     specifications described above because no other manufacturer has 
     implemented the highly parallel system architecture that provides the 
     high levels of memory bandwidth and I/O bandwidth (see specifications 
     1c and 1d) needed to endow the workstation with performance 
     characteristics equal to those of proprietary RISC/UNIX workstations."
     
     The situation hasn't changed one bit!  Compaq remains the leader in 
     the field when it comes to Windows NT/X86 technical workstations.  I 
     had many of the same concerns that you mentioned and have been more 
     than satisfied with the purchase I made a year ago.  As you probably 
     know, SPLUS has a nasty habit of scarfing up as much RAM as it can, 
     often leading to serious impairments in the performance of other 
     concurrently-run programs.  Purchasing a Compaq Professional 
     Workstation with dual Pentium II processors, lots of RAM, and the 
     highly-parallel system architecture all but eliminates performance 
     problems associated with multitasking.
     
     One more thing. I've listened to a lot of folks (even on this list 
     server!) moan and groan about Windows NT, citing how "unstable" and 
     "unreliable" it is as an operating system.  I've been using NT since 
     1992 (version 2.0, I think) and have not had a system crash in over 6 
     years.  I think I have taxed the operating system as much as anyone 
     and have yet to find a badly behaved application that can bring down 
     the entire operating system.  However, I suspect that the key to my 
     success (luck?) with Windows NT is that I have always purchased 
     computers with plenty of memory and hard disk space.  It's true that 
     NT can be criticized for its enormous memory requirements relative to 
     those of other OS's, such as UNIX or LINUX; however, the people that 
     are most disenchanted with Windows NT are probably using computers 
     that operate on the fringes of NT's actual requirements, which usually 
     exceed Microsoft's recommendations.
     
     Take a look at the technical specifications and statement of need in 
     the attached requisition.  Also, Compaq undoubtedly has newer models 
     by now with the faster processors, so visit their web site at 
     www.compaq.com.  Compaq provides lots of white papers that describe 
     the innovations introduced in their workstations, including the 
     highly-parallel system architecture.
     
     ---
     
     Harald Fekjær <hfe at math.uio.no>
     
