Recently I asked for help concerning the creation of two paramter gamma random
numbers. First I want to thank all, who helped me with their comments,
thank you
very much. Second I want to give a brief summary of the answers (11!).

Literature advice:

Luc Devroye: Non-Uniform Random Variate Generation, Springer-Verlag New
York ,1986 

Brian Ripley: Stochastic Simulation, Wiley Series in Probability and
Mathematical Statistics: Applied Probability and Statistics. John Wiley &
Sons, Inc., New York, 1987

George S. Fishman. His "Principles of Discrete Event Simulation" (Wiley, 1978)

James E. Gentle, Random Number Generation and Monte Carlo Methods, Springer,
1998

Johnson and Kotz (actually, Johnson, Kotz and Balakrishnan)
vol 1 of Continuous Univariate Distributions, chapter 17,
especially S. 17.5

www-sites:

The following site provides C and fortran procedures to create
random numbers:
http://odin.mdacc.tmc.edu/anonftp/.

A C++ library is found in
http://nz.com/webnz/robert/nzc_nr02.html

A very general generator is based on the cutting corner algorithm;
informations at
http://chenab.lums.edu.pk/~arifz/cutcorn.html

 
Finally I learned that a two paramter gamma density can easily be created
out of a one paramter gamma density as follows

1) the two parameter gamma is just a scale factor away from the one parameter
case. for example,

rgamma2 <- function(n, shape = stop("no mean arg"), mean = shape)
(.Internal(rgamma(n, par1 = shape), "S_rfuns", T, 6) * mean)/shape

is a two parameter gamma generator in a (shape, mean) parametrization.
compare to the standard rgamma() in S.

Thank you all again.

Jochen

 

