Thanks to all who replied.  Based on many responses, I see that I was not
alone in being unaware of write.table (some respondents did not know
what MS Word is!).  I forward this reply, thanks to Theo Brandsma (I
tried it, and it works!):

> Date: Fri, 5 Mar 1999 07:19:35 +0000
> From: Theo Brandsma <brandsma at knmi.nl>
> To: "Thomas J. Richards" <richards at toutatis.pci.upmc.edu>
> Subject: Re: [S] MS Word Tables
> 
> Dear Tom,
> 
> the answer is: use the write.table command.
> With this command you can write a matrix (or some other object) to 
> the clipboard (file='clipboard') and then copy it into WORD. 
> If you want to put data into an existing WORD table, you use sep='\t'
> and before copying into to WORD you select the corresponding rows and 
> colums in the WORD table. If you haven't a table yet you can use 
> sep=',' and then copy to WORD. Within WORD you then use the option 
> 'convert text into table' and you select the ',' as seperator.
> 
> Of the latter I give an an example below  for a matrix z:
> 
> write.table(round(z,1),file='clipboard',sep=',',dimnames.write=F,na=NA
> end.of.row='\n') 
> 
> Greetings,
> Theo
