I was previously using Splus 3.4 on SunOS 4 and have recently migrated to Splus 
5 on Linux.  I have quite a few gripes about the result, detailed below, but I 
think most of them are teething troubles with Splus 5 itself, rather than 
anything specific to Linux.  Specific problems:

1. If you follow to the letter the installation instructions dated November 
1998, they open up a security hole as big as a barn door.  (I reported this to 
MathSoft UK at the time, so I hope it will have been fixed by now.)  
Fortunately this will be obvious to most system administrators, and the 
workaround is obvious too.

2. I never particularly liked the Splus 3 graphical help system, but it was 
better than nothing.  That's what you've got now: nothing.  You're back to 
?command at the text prompt, which is a pain, and doesn't allow you to search 
for commands apropos of a particular subject.  (And thye don't give you an 
updated reference manual---even in electronic form---that you can browse 
through.)  There are some mentions in the documentation of a new SGML-based 
help system, but it doesn't seem to have made it to release 5.0

3. Splus AUDIT, with its invaluable Backtrack facility, has disappeared.  The 
.Audit file still exists, but you need to specify options(audit=1) for it to 
contain all the information it did in Splus 3.4.  Even then, some of the #~get 
statements that ought to be present aren't.  This is a serious step backwards,  
and has driven me to write a little Perl script that carries out the function 
of Backtrack but without using the (now incomplete) timetag information.

4. There seems to be a race condition: sometimes, if you've just typed in your 
nth command, say, and hit Return, the prompt for command n+1 comes back before 
the output from command n, and you then have to hit Return again to see the 
output from command n.  At the best of times this is a nuisance; if command n 
was something that wouldn't normally produce any output, but in fact produced 
an error message, then you will already have typed your next command before you 
see the error message.  This problem may have to do with the fact that I'm 
using a dual-processor machine.

5. Session initialisation doesn't appear to work quite in the way described in 
Sec. 4.1.1 of `Programming with Data' (John Chambers' new book, issued with the 
distribution), but the differences don't appear to be documented.

6. One thing I hoped would be improved in Splus 5.0, but hasn't been, is 
command line input and editing.  In particular you still can't use the arrow 
keys in command line editing and for searching through the command history, in 
the way you can in R.  Also, Splus still doesn't trouble to find out how wide 
is the text window in which it is operating.  You still need to tell it 
explicitly with options(width=130), say.  Even then, this is heeded only for 
data *output*: it will still do a disconcerting horizontal scroll if you try to 
type in a command more than about 70 characters long.  Another pain.

In summary, I think you'll have more trouble going from Splus 3.4 to 5.0 than 
going from Alpha to Linux.

Andrew Runnalls

