From rolf at math.unb.ca  Fri Mar  5 21:12:26 1999
From: rolf at math.unb.ca (Rolf Turner)
Date: Fri, 05 Mar 1999 17:12:26 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] a weird error message
In-Reply-To: <Pine.GSO.3.96L.990305131706.12925B-100000@moloch.stat.wisc.edu>
Message-ID: <199903052112.RAA11438@tanner.math.unb.ca>

Yuyun Jessie Yang writes:

>  In Splus 4.5 release 2 for window, we can use factorial() to calculate n!.
>  So, I did the following calculation and it errors.
>  
>  > n <- 12
>  > nr <- factorial(n)/(2^(n/2)*factorial(n/2))
>  > nr
>  [1] 10395
>  > P <- matrix(0, nrow=nr, ncol=n)
>  Error in dim(data) <- dim: Length of data (124740) doesn't match product
>   of dimensions (124728)

This is apparently another instance of numerical results being
approximate (and reals looking like integers) which has been thrashed
around recently vis-a-vis sqrt(25).  The gamma and factorial function
return real values which aren't quite exact since they result from
numerical calculations.

Try

> nr==10395
[1] F

If you look at nr - 10395 you get

[1] -1.63709e-11

So that even though nr prints as 10395 it isn't quite equal to it.

Using print(nr,digits=18) gives

[1] 10394.9999999999836

As an experiment I tried

> a <- 4 - 2e-11
> b <- 6
> P <- matrix(0,nrow=a,ncol=b)

and got

Error in dim(data) <- dim: Length of data (24) doesn't match product of
        dimensions (18)

The screw-up actually occurs within the function dim(), which is of the
``.Internal'' flavour.

The funny thing is that I found that setting

> a <- 4 + 2e-11  # Plus sign instead of minus
> b <- 6
> P <- matrix(0,nrow=a,ncol=b)

gives no error.  So a being epsilon less than an integer sets off
a bomb, but a being epsilon greater doesn't.  (I don't understand
that --- but I'm not very numerate!

Anyway the answer to Yuyun Jessie Yang's problem is to set
nr <- round(nr) and everything works.

Perhaps the code of factorial should be modified to apply
round() to its answer before it returns it.

                                        cheers,

                                                Rolf Turner
                                                rolf at math.unb.ca

P. S. All computations done under Splus 3.4 for Unix.  I rolled my own
factorial function (factorial <- function(n){gamma(n+1)}) in order
to reproduce Ms. Yang's example.
