From wvenable at attunga.stats.adelaide.edu.au  Sat Mar  6 05:29:48 1999
From: wvenable at attunga.stats.adelaide.edu.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Sat, 06 Mar 1999 15:59:48 +1030
Subject: Re: [S] Double Integration
In-Reply-To: <199903050316.DAA71194@out1.ibm.net>
References: <199903050316.DAA71194@out1.ibm.net>
Message-ID: <9903060529.AA05000@attunga.stats.adelaide.edu.au>

Tony Diaz asks:

> 
> Can somebody provide me with an illuminating sample implementation
> of double integration using the integrate() function in S-plus (in
> case it matters, I'm using v4.0). I am looking for an example with a
> more "general scope" than the one provided in the "help(integrate)"
> documentation. Specifically, I'd like to know how to setup the inner
> integral using lower and upper limits that are both general
> functions of the second variable.
> 
> Just to be sure, let me give a specific sample problem. Suppose
> 
>         myfun <- function(x,y){x+y}
> 
> which we want to integrate over the region
> 
>         1-y < x < y
>         .5 < y < 1.
> 
> How does one write an Splus function for the inner integral?

I find the example in the S+5.0 help file a bit misleading.  Here
is a single function in the style of that one that shows one way
of doing it.  

The important thing to remember is that the inner integral has to
be a function of two variables, *vectorized* in the sense that
given a vector of values for the outer variable of integration it
has to supply a vector of results for the inner integral, (if you
get my drift...)  This is what the unlist(lapply(...)) is all about.

double.integral.demo.fn <- function() {
  fun <- function(y) 
    unlist(lapply(y,
                  function(y) {
                    integrate(f = function(x, y) x+y,
                              lower = 1-y,
                              upper = y, 
                              y = y)$integral
                  }))
  integrate(f = fun,
            lower = 0.5,
            upper = 1)$integral
}

Here is a test run:

> double.integral.demo.fn()
[1] 0.33333

(It's not exactly very fast, though!)

Bill Venables

