> to make a difference. I still get the error that the object size isn't
> big enough. And the bigger I make the object.size, the bigger the
> "allocate" size that appears in the error message. The 5000000 limit
> seemed to be fixed no matter what I change the object.size to. Is that
> right, or am I not understanding the error and what the object.size
> is actually doing?
> 
> splus>options(object.size=6000000)
> splus>dust.dat <- scan("/export/home/ag/wave/regrid_rate_val2.dat",n = 
> 1048576)
> Problem in scan(file, character(), n): Cannot allocate 11535152 bytes: 
> options("object.size") is 6000000 
> Use traceback() to see the call stack

Try scanning the file in smaller pieces and then concatenating the vectors.
You will need an object size of more than 8M just to store that object.

> options(object.size=10^7)
for(i in 1:10) 
  assign(paste("tt",i,sep=""), scan("tmp", n=10^5, skip=i*10^5),
         immediate=T)
> ttt <- c(tt1,tt2,tt3,tt4,tt5,tt6,tt7,tt8,tt9,tt10)

works for me on a modest (64Mb Ultra 1) machine without paging on
S-PLUS 5.0r3.  (tmp is 10^6 numbers, one per line. Adjust accordingly.)

