Hi,

Sorry, I am on UNIX system.
I have got solutions to my problem on mathematical symbols and here are some of 
the answers:

Regards to all those that responded.

S.D. Byers:

text(x.pos, y.pos,"a",font=13)

will give you an alpha at (x.pos,y.pos).

Note it may not appear on the device, but will appear when you print it to
a ps file.

Here is a useful function to quickly prevu what you have done:

##------------------------------------------------------

prevu<-function(width = 6, horizontal = F) {
#
# Function to get a postscript preview of one's plot.
#
        if(names(dev.list())[[1]] == "X11") {
                printgraph(file = "/tmp/test.ps", width = 
width,horizontal=horizontal)
                unix("ghostview /tmp/test.ps &")
        }
        if(names(dev.list())[[1]] == "motif") {
                printgraph(file = "/tmp/test.ps",horizontal=horizontal)
                unix.shell("ghostview /tmp/test.ps &")
        }
}

Alan Zaslavsky:

This is a definite FAQ - shows up about once every year.  Here are several
solutions:

(1) for a single Greek character: use the "symbol" font (Font 13 on Unix
systems, I don't know about the PCs).

(2) if it suits your needs and you are on a PC, use the graph editing
features.

(3) if you need to mix things up in one label, use my "postscriptfonts"
archive from Statlib. THese functions allow you to create simple to
moderately complex labels using a string with escape sequences for change
of font, super and subscripts, change of size, etc.  Takes some fiddling
to make it beautiful but it works entirely within S-plus.

(4) use the "psfrag" utilities (there are a few variations around) that
work with LaTeX or TeX, so you can create the label in those languages and
have them appear within a PostScript plot.  THe way to go for truly
high-quality typesettnig, but requires some additional steps.

Ellis:

You don't say what system you are on. If it's Windows version 4, you can
create editable graphs with Options|Graph Options and click Create
Editable Objects. You can then for example double click on the x axis
and modify the text on the screen. I just tried using the superscript
and subscript buttons and then changing to Math1 font to get Greek
characters. (In TeX-speak I changed typed $a_b^c \delta$

How do you do this on the command line? Look in the history log to find
the gui command for what you just did with the mouse, then modify as
appropriate. For instance, my tinkering was equivalent to this gui
command:

guiModify( "CommentDate", Name = "GSD2$1$1", Title =
"a]b[[c]\\\"Math1\"d")

Andreas Krause:

So here's an easy solution, if you really just want to put a Greek letter in a
graph on a UNIX system. Try
text(0, 0, "abc", font=13)
This gives you alpha beta gamma at the position 0, 0.

