From tsmith at whsun1.wh.whoi.edu  Tue Mar  9 19:40:10 1999
From: tsmith at whsun1.wh.whoi.edu (Tim Smith)
Date: Tue, 09 Mar 1999 14:40:10 -0500
Subject: [S] problem with points.in.poly
Message-ID: <36E5791A.6B07FF47@whsun1.wh.whoi.edu>

The function in the spatial module points.in.poly has a documented bug
that sounded innocent to me, namely:

BUG
if a ray from a point to an edge intersects a horizontal edge, i.e. is
collinear with it, the C program will return TRUE even if such point is
not in the polygon.

In fact, the function also returns FALSE for points in the polygon that
have y values exactly equal to nodes of the polygon with y coordinates
less than the maximum and greater than the minimum. 

pip.test() displays the results for the simplest case where it arises,
namely a triangle, where green indicates points.in.poly returned T and
purple that it returned F.

pip.test(x.poly=c(.5,1,2,1,2,.5),y.poly=c(.3,2,2,1,.5,.3))

shows that the pattern is a little more complex. 

The problem seems to arise from exactly equal y values, and I've fixed
it for the cases I've been considering by adding an amount to the
polygon nodes that is smaller than the level of precision of the
latitude values, thus avoiding the possibility of exact equivalence. I'm
not sure if this is a general fix, and would appreciate any other
experience that folks may have had with this or some other points in a
polygon algorithm.

However, discovering the problem was not easy.  It would be helpful if
the documentation were corrected and made more clear what is happening,
and perhaps to have the Splus function points.in.poly or pip that calls
the C routine could include a fix or at least a flag of a potential
problem if such equivalences exist.

Tim Smith

function(x.poly=c(.5,1,1.5,1,2,0),y.poly=c(0,2,1,0))
{# evaluates points.in.poly for sampling of points within
# enclosing polygon

x_rep(seq(0,2,.1),21)
y_rep(seq(0,2,.1),1,each=21)
z_points.in.poly(x,y,list(x=x.poly,y=y.poly))

plot(x,y,type='n')

lines(x.poly,y.poly,col=4)

points(x[!z],y[!z],col=3)
points(x[z],y[z],col=4)
}

