Date: Wed, 10 Mar 1999 12:02:32 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] writing standard splus
In-Reply-To: <Pine.OSF.3.95.990310145125.5377A-100000@einstein.math.sci.qut.edu.au>
References: <Pine.OSF.3.95.990310145125.5377A-100000@einstein.math.sci.qut.edu.au>
Message-ID: <14054.42408.348745.166725@grouper.stat.ufl.edu>

In message 
<Pine.OSF.3.95.990310145125.5377A-100000 at einstein.math.sci.qut.edu.au> you 
write:
> 
> I can write Splus functions with the form myfunction(data, argument1,
> argument). This time I have to write some functions for other people to
> use. I just wonder how I can write a function with a model formula. 

Zhaorong,

As Brian Ripley suggested, you probably will want to imitate the built
in modeling functions of Splus.  Unfortunately it may take a while to
figure out how they work.  When I was learning to do this I created a
heavily commented version of the standard glm function (this is a
couple of years old, so you might want to check that the glm function
hasn't been changed).  In case it might help you, I've attached a copy
below.  You may also find it helpful to compare this with other
examples, including the lm and gam functions and any examples in
Venables and Ripley, though as Ripley says, the functions provided by
Splus sometimes seem inconsistent in their approach.  Reading the
documentation for things like model.frame, model.matrix, match.call,
etc. may also be helpful.

Once you've gotten your modeling function written, you might also want
to creating methods for plot, summary, and so forth.  V&R probably
have the most useful explanation of this sort of thing.

Good luck.

Brett

######################################################################
### glm function with comments added by Brett Presnell
### (presnell at stat.ufl.edu).
######################################################################
"glm" <-
  function(formula = formula(data), family = gaussian, data = sys.parent(), 
           weights, subset, na.action, start = eta,
           control = glm.control(...), method = "glm.fit", model = F,
           x = F, y = T, contrasts = NULL, ...)
{
  ######################################################################
  ## THE FIRST SECTION IS FAIRLY GENERIC S MODELING STUFF
  ######################################################################
  ##
  ## The call is saved to be returned as part of the output.
  ##
  call <- match.call()
  ##
  ## Save the call again, but this time for internal purposes.
  ## We don't expand the dots because whatever is in there will
  ## not be used in the when we create the model frame below.
  ##
  m <- match.call(expand = F)
  ##
  ## The next line nullifies some arguments before evaluating
  ## model.frame on the remaining arguments (if they are specified in
  ## the call): formula, data, weights, subset, na.action, and
  ## start. Did I miss anything?
  ##
  ## Note that match.call returns an object with mode "call", which is
  ## something like a list with first component being the function name.
  ## The remaining components are the arguments actually specified in
  ## the function call.  In particular, if an argument has default values
  ## and the call doesn't override it, then match.call doesn't doesn't
  ## return it as part of its value.
  ##
  ## BEGIN WHINGING ABOUT OBSCURE CODE
  ## This behaviour of match.call, coupled with lazy evaluation,
  ## explains why the author(s) of this function can get away with
  ## "start=eta" whether eta exists or not.  Note that even if eta
  ## exists as an object in the parent frame it is ignored unless the
  ## user has specified "start=eta" explicitly in the call to glm.
  ## This seems unnecessarily obtuse.  Why not just have "start=NULL"
  ## to avoid any possible confusion?  The only reason I can see for
  ## "start=eta" is as a sort of internal documentation, in that it
  ## indicates that "start" represents starting values for the linear
  ## predictor, but this of course assumes familiarity with the notation
  ## of say McCullagh and Nelder.
  ## END WHINGING
  ##
  m$family <- m$method <- m$model <- m$x <- m$y <- m$control <-
    m$contrasts <- m$... <- NULL
  ##
  ## Replace "glm" with "model.frame" in what is left of the call.
  ##
  m[[1]] <- as.name("model.frame")
  ##
  ## Now evaluate m to create the data frame for further work.
  ##
  m <- eval(m, sys.parent())
  ##
  ## This extracts and save the terms attribute, which is later used
  ## to create the model matrix.
  ##
  Terms <- attr(m, "terms")
  ##
  ## It seems that the next line should logically go before the
  ## previous one, since here we just return the frame, in which case
  ## Terms is not used.
  ##
  if(method == "model.frame")
    return(m)
  ##
  ## Interesting.  The attributes of the model frame are extracted and
  ## stored in "a", but "a" never seems to be used.  Am I missing
  ## anything here?
  ##
  a <- attributes(m)
  ##
  ## Extract the response, the X matrix, and the weights from the
  ## model frame.
  ##
  Y <- model.extract(m, response)
  X <- model.matrix(Terms, m, contrasts)
  w <- model.extract(m, weights)
  ##
  ## If no weights are specified, set all weights to 1.  Also issue
  ## an error message and stop in case of any negative weights.
  ##
  if(!length(w))
    w <- rep(1, nrow(m))
  else if(any(w < 0))
    stop("negative weights not allowed")
  ##
  ## Extract the starting values for the linear predictor.
  ##
  start <- model.extract(m, start)
  ##
  ## Extract the offset, which must be specified in the model formula.
  ## Note that this is just a term who's regression coefficient is
  ## known (or assumed) to be 1.
  ##
  offset <- model.extract(m, offset)
  ##
  ######################################################################
  ## THIS SECTION IS MORE GLM SPECIFIC
  ######################################################################
  ##
  ## Get the distributional family of the response and the fitting
  ## method to be used.
  ##
  family <- as.family(family)
  if(missing(method))
    method <- attr(family, "method")
  if(!is.null(method)) {
    if(!exists(method, mode = "function"))
      stop(paste("unimplemented method:", method))
  }
  else method <- "glm.fit"
  glm.fitter <- get(method)
  ## 
  ## Compute the fit by calling a more bare bones fitting routine
  ## where the real computations are done.
  ## 
  fit <-
    glm.fitter(x = X, y = Y, w = w, start = start, offset = offset, 
               family = family, maxit = control$maxit, epsilon = control$
               epsilon, trace = control$trace, null.dev = T, ...)       #
  ##
  ## If an offset and intercept is present, iterations are needed to
  ## compute the Null deviance; these are done here, unless the model
  ## is NULL, in which case the computations have been done already
  ##
  if(any(offset) && attr(Terms, "intercept")) {
    null.deviance <-
      if(length(Terms))
        glm.fitter(X[,"(Intercept)", drop = F], Y,
                   w, offset = offset, family = family,
                   maxit = control$maxit, epsilon = control$epsilon,
                   null.dev = NULL)$deviance
      else fit$deviance
    fit$null.deviance <- null.deviance
  }
  ######################################################################
  ## THE LAST SECTION IS AGAIN FAIRLY GENERIC
  ######################################################################
  ##
  ## Attach other desired information about the fit before returning.
  ##
  attr(fit, "class") <- c("glm", "lm")
  fit$terms <- Terms
  fit$formula <- as.vector(attr(Terms, "formula"))
  fit$call <- call
  if(model)
    fit$model <- m
  if(x)
    fit$x <- X
  if(!y)
    fit$y <- NULL
  fit
}
