Subject: 
Message-ID: <Chameleon.921104946.mueter@sole>

I received several responses to a question posed last week regarding the limit
on the number of vertices that polygon will plot. As two respondents pointed 
out the polygon help file contains the followig warning which I overlooked! 
(so much for warnings!):

WARNING:
       Some graphics devices impose a  limit  on  the  number  of
       vertices  there  can  be  in  a  polygon.  Some PostScript
       printers,  for  instance,  have  a  limit  of  about  1500
       vertices.

Here's several work arounds:
- If looks isn't an issue the polygon can be filled with high density lines
  instead, which seems to work without the same limitations.

- Subsampling the vertices (e.g. picking every 2nd or 3d point) until the
  number of points is less than 1500. In most cases this can be done without   

  too much loss of information as 1500 points create a reasonably smooth
  outline.

- Dividing the polygon into smaller polygons, which is easy to achieve in some 

  cases, but is difficult to automate because of the need for the line
  connecting the first and last vertices of the "sub-polygon" to not intersect
  the rest of the hull. 

Thanks to Henrik Aalborg Nielsen, Anne York, and Andreas Krause

original query:

> Franz Mueter wrote:
> 
> When I draw filled polygons that are defined by several 1000 points and 
write
> the output to a postscript file, part of the polygon is not filled. It seems
> as if the first 1500 or so points are ignored and only the remaining part of
> the polygon is filled. Any idea how to fix this (besides dividing it up into
> smaller polygons)??
> 
> Thanks
> 
