Subject: [S] Low Rank estimation methods, large data sets, generalised linear models
Message-ID: <41C485682BF4D011964400805F89E42A147ED8@OCTOPUS>

  Dear all,

We are having an "interesting" time trying to estimate parameters for
Multinomial Logit (MNL) models using Ripley's multinom, and S-Plus'
inherent glm binomial logit. Both have presented problems.
Both appear to have trouble with low rank data sets (more columns than
rows). In addition, MNL does not work with data sets of a serious
magnitude (~100 columns).

While we have solutions to these problems external to Splus, we would
like to stay with the tool.

It appears that the first limitation is due to the use of the IWLS
algorithm (derived from linearity considerations not necessarily
applicable to a generalised linear model). 

Questions :

Is code available to compute these methods using ML , iterated gradient
descent, conjugate gradients or a related method that is not affected by
low rank that can also handle data sets of industrial size ?

