> It appears that the first limitation is due to the use of the IWLS
> algorithm (derived from linearity considerations not necessarily
> applicable to a generalised linear model). 
> 
> Questions :
> 
> Is code available to compute these methods using ML , iterated gradient
> descent, conjugate gradients or a related method that is not affected by
> low rank that can also handle data sets of industrial size ?

Oh dear, so many conceptual errors! I will desist from pointing out all the
problems.  Just one will do.  By Cover (1965), for logistic regression
there is no ML solution for less than p+2 cases, and unlikely to be one for
less than 2p cases, where p is the number of regressors (after expanding
coded variables). If you really want to do this, the best fit is a perfect
one (you don't need ML, and the likelihood has no maximum) and can be found
by linear programming methods at close to the known lower bound on
computational efficiency.  For the multinomial case see a good book on the
subject, and in any case do learn some statistical theory before
denigrating software for not solving an impossible problem.

Both the statements about multinom are AFAIK wrong. It has no limits (but
does have a safety catch) and does not use IWLS.

> Eugene Dubossarsky
> eugened at ntf.com.au
> Nelson Taylor Fox
> Leaders in Decision Support Solutions

Including solving the insoluble, I see. Leaders in arrogance?

