From pmbutler at gsbphd.uchicago.edu  Fri Mar 12 17:36:40 1999
From: pmbutler at gsbphd.uchicago.edu (Marty Butler)
Date: Fri, 12 Mar 1999 12:36:40 -0500
Subject: [S] Summary: S-PLUS 4.5 Professional v. Stata 6.0
Message-ID: <4.1.19990312123435.00942190@gsbphd.uchicago.edu>

Thank you to all who responded to my query about how S-PLUS 4.5, Stata 6.0, and
LIMDEP 7.0 compare to one another, especially Marc R. Feldesman, Frank Harrell,
and an individual who wishes to remain anonymous.  My original post and the
replies I received are shown below.

Original Post
-------------
I'm looking at purchasing a statistical software package and could benefit 
from the groups' expertise. In particular, could someone please summarize 
the basic differences between S-PLUS 4.5 Professional and Stata 6.0 (i.e., 
advantages/disadvantages of each relative to one another)? Which other 
statistical software packages (for Windows) exist, if any, whose 
performance compares favorably to that of these packages? 

Also, does LIMDEP 7.0 offer significant capabilities incremental to S-PLUS 
or Stata when dealing with problems involving limited dependent variables? 
If so, could you describe generally what they are?

Thanks for the help--I'll summarize the responses I receive and post 
them to both groups.

Dr. Marc R. Feldesman <feldesmanm at pdx.edu>
------------------------------------------
Both are programmable, but I believe S-Plus is more extendable - it 
is object-oriented, which can be both a blessing and a curse..

S-Plus' graphics are infinitely better than anything Stata can 
possibly do.

Stata is excellent for econometric and epidemiologic work; S-Plus is 
good at these too. S-Plus, however, is exceptional in both 
conventional and modern multivariate techniques, whereas Stata is 
very limited in the multivariate routines. Stata, for example, 
doesn't do any discriminant analysis or manova, while S-Plus does 
both. Stata has a very limited implementation of cluster analysis, 
while S-Plus has a rich implementation. Stata does little in the way 
of "robust" statistical analysis, while S-Plus excels in robust 
analysis.

Both programs have fairly steep learning curves, particularly if you 
are not proficient at programming. Both programs have made strides 
to make their programs more "user friendly" but to take advantage of 
the power of either, you really do need to be able to program. I 
found S-Plus' programmability a more understandable paradigm than 
Stata's, even though both are organized around C/C++. Stata's 
paradigm is more oriented toward the C end -- not object oriented -- 
while S-Plus is organized more toward the C++ (and hence object 
oriented) end.

Others to consider are SPSS 9.0, Systat 8.0, and NCSS 2000. All are 
general purpose statistical packages with much easier (well, not so 
true with SAS) to use interfaces, but with powerful programming 
capabilities that lie below the surface. NCSS 2000 is completely 
menu-driven but has an extraordinary range of routines. If you don't 
want to do programming at all, this would be an inexpensive but 
comprehensive solution to your needs.

SPSS and Systat are at www.spss.com 
SAS is at www.sas.com 
NCSS is at www.ncss.com

Hope this helps

Frank E. Harrell Jr. <fharrell at virginia.edu> 
--------------------------------------------
It may help to look at the review Rich Goldstein and I had in The American 
Statistician about a 1.5 years ago, which reviewd Stata, Splus, Limdep, 
and others for survival analysis capabilities. 

[For those interested, the article cite follows: Harrell, Frank E., Jr., and
Richard Goldstein.  1997.  "A Survey of Microcomputer Survival Analysis
Software: The Need for an Integrated Framework."  The American Statistician,
Vol. 51, No. 4, pp. 360?373.  -mbb]

Anonymous
---------
I've used all three, and here are my quick evaluations:

- Stata is the best all around package of the three (read: it will 
do 90% of everything most folks would want to do, in the most consistent 
and user-friendly way of the lot). Particularly compared to LIMDEP, 
its estimation algorithms and convergence are, I've found, much 
smoother and more consistent than the others.

- S-Plus has a slight edge in terms of survival analysis, 
nonparametrics/data smoothing, and a few other things (e.g. GRAPHICS 
-- OK, a large edge in terms of graphics). BUT S-Plus is about to 
release version 5.0 for Windows (or at least, that's what they tell 
me; the UNIX version is already out). So all bets are off for 
comparison until that's available (I suppose one could go look at the 
features for the unix version, but I haven't done so yet).

- LIMDEP used to have a large edge in terms of limited dependent 
variables. Stata has now closed that gap, though there are still 
some very useful things (nested logits, multinomial probits, 
heterosscedastic ordered probits) which LIMDEP will do that Stata 
won't.

- As for other packages, there's always SAS (huge, cumbersome, but 
very versatile), and GAUSS, and probably others that I'm not thinking 
of at the moment.

If I had to live with one, it'd be Stata. With two, its a tossup 
over which I'd add: S-Plus if survival and graphics were important, 
LIMDEP if discrete LHS variables were crucial.

Hope this helps.

