From: ckeller at aicos.com (Christian Keller)
Date: Wed, 17 Mar 1999 17:16:19 +0100
Subject: Re: [S] subsetting a data frame with multiple measurement
References: <36EF8C65.6016C82B@telecomplus.sn>
Message-ID: <36EFD553.38E0DE06@aicos.com>

Renaud Lancelot <lancelot at telecomplus.sn> wrote:

>> Dear S-Plus users,
>> 
>> I have a data frame with multiple measurements, i.e.
>> measurements are repeated at different occasions on
>> the same id. I want to select the lines for which id
>> appears at least n times. How should I do ?
>>
>> ...

Use one of the following functions (and see the example below)

select1 <- function(id, n){
  z <- as.variable(factor(id, labels=table(id)))
  z>=n
}

select2 <- function(id, n){
  ord <- order(id)
  r <- rle(id[ord])$lengths
  z <- rep(r, r)
  z[ord] <- z
  z>=n
}

Example: data frame 'fuel.frame'
-------
  
> summary(fuel.frame)
      Weight         Disp.          Mileage     
 Min.   :1845   Min.   : 73.0   Min.   :18.00  
 1st Qu.:2571   1st Qu.:113.8   1st Qu.:21.00  
 Median :2885   Median :144.5   Median :23.00  
 Mean   :2901   Mean   :152.1   Mean   :24.58  
 3rd Qu.:3231   3rd Qu.:180.0   3rd Qu.:27.00  
 Max.   :3855   Max.   :305.0   Max.   :37.00  
       Fuel            Type   
 Min.   :2.703   Compact:15  
 1st Qu.:3.704   Large  : 3  
 Median :4.348   Medium :13  
 Mean   :4.210   Small  :13  
 3rd Qu.:4.762   Sporty : 9  
 Max.   :5.556   Van    : 7

Select all rows in 'fuel.frame' for which the levels of the
variable 'Type' appear at least 10 times. 

X <- fuel.frame[select1(fuel.frame$Type, 10), ]

> summary(X$Type)
 Compact Large Medium Small Sporty Van 
      15     0     13    13      0   0

> all.equal(select1(fuel.frame$Type, 10),
            select2(fuel.frame$Type, 10))
[1] T

I hope this is correct and is what you wanted.

