Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.04.9903240946510.8308-100000@param.math.umbc.edu>

Thanks to all the helpful people who answered re: the disappearance of
help.start() from Splus 5 Version. The answer was not very encouraging,
which means that help.start() seems to have well and truly disappeared,
at least for now.

Here are the first three helpful suggestions:

Terry Therneau:

  The command isn't there.  The 5.0 kernel has new features to handle
auxillary processes, including windows and such, and help.start just hasn't
been ported.  Statsci would need to give the definitive answer on "when"
this will be done.

Brian Ripley:

   Online help for this release of S-PLUS 5.0 for UNIX is a
   hybrid of the old nroff-based help and a new SGML-based help system.

My understanding is that a different browseable help system will be
available in later versions of 5.x.

Christian Keller:

In the release notes (Version 5.0 Release 3) it reads:

> The help.start function (and the associated X11 help window
> system) is no longer available.
>
> Online help for this release of S-PLUS 5.0 for UNIX is a
> hybrid of the old nroff-based help and a new SGML-based help
> system.
>
> The S-PLUS User's Guide, Guide to Statistics, and the S-PLUS
> Installation and Maintenance Guide are all available online
> as PDF files.

However, in the unix man page (file $SHOME/doc/manl/Splus.1)
there are still the following lines:

> An X11-based help system is available through the
> .I help.start
> function.
> ..
> .SH EXAMPLES
> .nf
> help.start()  # starts X11 help window showing all
>                 S-PLUS functions

which is definitively NOT true anymore!

If you are interested, you may also take a look at John
Chambers Web-Site ("Programming with Data, A Guide to the S
Language") at

http://cm.bell-labs.com/cm/ms/departments/sia/Sbook/Documentation/index.html

