From jwhite at bach.bio.ri.ccf.org  Thu Mar 25 13:27:03 1999
From: jwhite at bach.bio.ri.ccf.org (Jennifer White)
Date: Thu, 25 Mar 1999 08:27:03 -0500
Subject: [S] axes for histograms
Message-ID: <199903251327.IAA13456@franck.biostats>

I want to thank the group for the speedy and numerous responses to my query:

'I need to produce histograms that conform to a certain appearance for 
publication. The basic hist() plot does not have x and y axes meeting at the 
origin (my plots are simple and satisfactory in all other respects).'

My original code was:

>hist(valves.ce$yr, breaks=seq(1976,1998,1), axes=F,
      xlab="Dates of CE Usage", ylab="Number",ylim=c(0,100))      
>axis(side=1, at=seq(1976,1998,1), lab=seq(1977,1999,1))
>axis(side=2, at=seq(0,100,25), lab=seq(0,100,25)) 

Many responses pointed to the solution of using box(). However, I needed to 
simply fill in the break at the origin. The solution that worked perfectly for 
me was given as:

# Add the following code 
>par("usr")
>abline(h=par("usr")[3])
>abline(v=par("usr")[1])

Thanks to Peter Perkins at UCSD.

Thanks to everyone else who helped elucidate arguments to par(). I am impressed 
with the amount and quality of information I received. 

