Hello,

As promised, here is a summary of the responses I received to my inquiry
about postscript printing.  I received responses from Nick Ellis and
Madeline Bauer.  Although neither response provided a fix for my problem, I
learned something from both.  Thanks Nick and Madeline.  Their responses,
with my original post are included below.  One more note, I've started to
think that my problem relates to ps.preamble in some way.  If I use
different postscript drivers I get different results, and if I edit the
default ps.preamble that is called by postscript() I also get different
results.  Unfortunately, I must admit that I'm pretty clueless about how I
might edit ps.preamble to get what I want.  Thanks again.

***************************************************************
>>From Nick Ellis:

Just one more thing you could try is send the .ps file directly to the
printer, bypassing GSView, assuming it is a postscript printer. On my NT
system this is done in DOS by

> copy file.ps lpt1:

having previously set the lpt1 port with the command

> net use lpt1: \\machinename\printername /persistent:no

***************************************************************
>>From Madeline Bauer

I have some suggestions, but these may be irrelevant, since I use SPlus 3.3
in win 3.1, but here goes:

I have added a printer selection in windows print manager which specifies
the output to a file. Here I can select the dpi (300, 600, 1200) and
choose postscript or encapsulated postscript.

Then, in SPlus 3.3, I select this "printer" as my default.

now, when I'm ready to create a plot file, I issue the trellis.device
command:

trellis.device ( win.printer, file="myplot.ps" )

I also fiddle around with the arguments width, height, orientation, and
pointsize.

When I want a hard copy I either use GSview or simply issue a DOS command.

I use this extensively, particularly to create EPS plot files which I
import into PageMaker, where I can "size" the plot to whatever looks
o.k.!

-----Original Message-----
From: George Watters [mailto:GWatters at iattc.ucsd.edu]
Sent: Tuesday, March 23, 1999 5:15 PM
To: 'S-news'
Subject: [S] postscript printing

Dear S crowd,

I know there are many FAQ's on various postscript topics, but I haven't been
able to figure out my problem despite searching the S-news Archives and
looking at all the various documentation I have.  So please bear with me if
this is already common knowledge....

The preliminaries:  I use Splus Ver. 4.5 Pro. Rel. 2 in Windows NT 4.0; I
print to a networked Lexmark Optra S PS2.

I've been trying to print some trellis graphics, and the resolution of the
printed result is not very good.  I'm plotting hundreds of small filled
squares (using polygon() inside a panel function) that have specific sizes,
but the squares are printed as rectangles.  As far as I know, this is not a
problem with my aspect ratio (which I set to a predetermined value in the
call that creates my trellis).  I came to this conclusion by the following
hints:

1) If I change the size of the squares (by changing the points input to
polygon()) but leave the aspect ratio constant, the squares become more or
less rectangular. 

2) The help file for postscript() contains the following warning...

"Some postscript printers may not have the same number of rasters per inch
in both the horizontal and vertical directions.  If you are trying to do
careful geometric drawings or trying to match the scale of your plot with
other plots with known scales this can cause problems."

3) If I open the postscript() device (ignoring trellis.device() for now) and
print the .ps file through GSView (Ver. 2.6) the squares are square.  This
isn't a solution though -- without using trellis.device() none of the text
(axis labels etc.) accompanies the plot.

4) If I send output to the printer using syntax like

        trellis.device(win.printer, printer.type="postscript")
        my.plot()
        dev.off()

the squares are rectangles.

5) The squares are also rectangles if I use syntax like

        trellis.device(postscript, file="myplot.ps")
        my.plot()
        dev.off()

and then print myplot.ps through GSView.  Although trellis.device() has the
"..." argument and postscript() has an argument called "rasters" (that
increases resolution for postscript devices), I can't use rasters as an
argument to trellis.device().

So, does anyone have some advice?  Am I calling trellis.device()
incorrectly?  Is there a workaround for using postscript() without
trellis.device() that retains the axis labels?  Thanks in advance for any
help you might be able to offer.  I'm happy to summarize any help I receive
for the group.

George

