From ckeller at aicos.com  Mon Mar 29 09:41:20 1999
From: ckeller at aicos.com (Christian Keller)
Date: Mon, 29 Mar 1999 11:41:20 +0200
Subject: Re: [S] Difficulty with merge
References: <199903290616.QAA14058@ornsun.agric.nsw.gov.au>
Message-ID: <36FF4AC0.5B5C4252@aicos.com>

Remy vande Ven <vandevr at ornsun.agric.nsw.gov.au> wrote:
 
>> Can someone offer a solution to my problem?
>> 
>> I am using Splus 5.0 on a unix machine and have a
>> problem with the "merge" function when trying to merge
>> two dataframes.
>> 
>> ...
>>   
>> S> t1
>>               Dist         Species SurvPct 
>> 1 NSW Central Zone       wild oats     100
>> 2 NSW Central Zone        Capeweed     100
>> 3 NSW Central Zone saffron thistle      67
>>   
>> S> t2
>>               Dist   Species TQAPct 
>> 1 NSW Central Zone  ryegrass     50
>> 2 NSW Central Zone wild oats     39
>> 
>> S> merge(t1,t2,by=c("Dist","Species"),all=T)
>> Problem in [.data.frame: Missing value where logical
>>                          needed: 
>> if(!missing(..1) && mode(..1) != "missing" &&
>>    is.null(dup.row.names) && !is.logical(i) &&
>>    i[1] > 0 && any(duplicated(i)))
>>             rows <- make.names(rows, unique = T) 
>> Use traceback() to see the call stack
>> 
>> This error message tell _me_ nothing. Maybe someone
>> else can decipher it.

This looks like a bug in  [.data.frame  when unique row
names are constructed.                   

Here is a small example:

> a <- data.frame(x=1:2, y=3:4)
> a
  x y 
1 1 3
2 2 4

> options(error=dump.frames)                  
> a[c(NA,1,1),]
Problem in [.data.frame: Missing value where logical needed:
            if(!missing(..1) && mode(..1) != "missing" &&
               is.null(dup.row.names) && !is.logical(i) &&
               i[1] > 0 && any(duplicated(i)))
            rows <- make.names(rows, unique = T)
Evaluation frames saved in object "last.dump", use debugger()
to examine them
> debugger()
Message: Problem in [.data.frame: Missing value where logical
         needed:
        if(!missing(..1) && mode(..1) != "missing" &&
           is.null(dup.row.names) && !is.logical(i) &&
           i[1] > 0 && any(duplicated(i)))
                     rows <- make.names(rows, unique = T)
browser: Frame 11 
b([.data.frame)> ..1
[1] NA  1 1
b([.data.frame)> i
[1] NA  1 1
b([.data.frame)> dup.row.names
NULL
b([.data.frame)> c
NULL                   

The error occurs when the index vector (i.e. 'i') has
duplicated values (as in c(NA,1,1)) and i[1] == NA.
Then the expression in if() is NA.

Btw. S-PLUS 3.4 gives
> a[c(NA,1,1),]
     x  y 
  X NA NA
 X1  1  3
X11  1  3

By studying the help file help(data.frame) you learn that
there is a new argument 'dup.row.names' in data.frame():

>> VALUE:
>>  ...
>>                                       It may also have an
>>  attribute "dup.row.names", which, if non-null, indicates
>>  that row names need not be unique.

Now:
  
> a <- data.frame(x=1:2, y=3:4, dup.row.names=T)
> a[c(NA,1,1),]
   x  y 
  NA NA
1  1  3
1  1  3

Finally, here is a solution to your original problem:
  
t1 <- data.frame(Dist=rep("NSW Central Zone",3),
                 Species=c("wild oats","Capeweed",
                           "saffron thistle"),
                 SurvPct=c(100,100,67))

t2 <- data.frame(Dist=rep("NSW Central Zone",2),
                 Species=c("ryegrass","wild oats"),
                 TQAPct=c(50,39))

> attr(t1, "dup.row.names") <- T
> attr(t2, "dup.row.names") <- T

> merge(t1, t2, by=c("Dist","Species"), all=T)
              Dist         Species SurvPct TQAPct 
1 NSW Central Zone        Capeweed     100     NA
2 NSW Central Zone        ryegrass      NA     50
3 NSW Central Zone saffron thistle      67     NA
4 NSW Central Zone       wild oats     100     39

  
