From michael.m.meyer at boeing.com  Mon Mar 29 14:57:08 1999
From: michael.m.meyer at boeing.com (Michael Meyer)
Date: Mon, 29 Mar 1999 06:57:08 -0800
Subject: Re: [S] Importing, Editing functions
In-Reply-To: <ripley@stats.ox.ac.uk>
References: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9903280707090.18128-100000@auk.stats>
Message-ID: <199903291457.GAA16937@desire.rt.cs.boeing.com>

If you are EXPORTING data/functions from Splus-5 and want it to be 
readable from within an earlier version of Splus (like 3.4), then
        data.dump(..., old=T)
will certainly help.  Without the "old=T" argument, even simple data 
will not be readable within earlier version of Splus.  With the "old=T" 
version, you can at least read the file.

 --Mike

>***> Prof Brian D Ripley wrote (Sun, 28 Mar 1999 07:16:06 +0100)
> 
> On Sat, 27 Mar 1999, Lance Hoffmeyer wrote (in one long line):
> 
> > I am using S5 in Linux with ESS and am trying to import a function
>              ^^  S-PLUS 5, I presume.
> > I wrote in S4 Win95.  How can I do this?
> 
> Use dump() and source() or (better) data.dump() and data.restore().
> See the help pages for details.  Each provides a platform-independent
> representation of S(-PLUS) objects.
> 
> Going the other way needs a little more care: it is possible to have
> S-PLUS 5 objects (even functions) that cannot be parsed in S-PLUS 3/4.
> This is unlikely to happen without your deliberating using the new
> features of S version 4.
> 
> S5: now there's a thought!
