Subject: [S] Alternative functions for numerical minimization of a user-suppl
Message-ID: <199903301603.LAA24007@sarah.albany.edu>

New to  S+, I am  using a large data set (27,000 observations 
with 40+ variables) to obtain GMM and maximum likelihood 
estimates for some somewhat  complicated (or messy)  limited 
dependent variables models.   I'm using S+ 4.5 for windows.

My question concerns the availability of functions for 
numerical maximization or minimization of user supplied 
differentiable functions, other than the standard ones in S+ 
(i.e., ms, nls, nlmin and nlminb).  ms and nls aren't helpful because 
I can't set the problem up as a simple sum of nonlinear functions 
across observations.   The quasi-Newton algorithm (nlmin)  does 
not allow me to specify the analytic first derivatives.  This results 
in the numerical optimization being very slow, given the complexity 
of the model and the large number of paramters.  (Calculating 
analytic derivates is faster than calculating numerical derivatives 
by a factor of 15-20.) Furthermore, I have found that impercision in 
the numerical derivatives result in nlmin stopping when the parameter 
vector is somewhat close to, but not at, the function maximum.  The 
function nlminb which does allow the specification of analytic 
derivates takes care of the second problem (false convergence)  but  
has proven to be unbelievably slow!  

     Is there a  general, user friendly, numerical minimixation
program in S+  which is generally useful for maximumlikelihood and 
GMM problems.  (The comparable function in GAUSS is Maxlik.  There is 
a fortram based package called GQOPT.)  I would like a program that 
provides  great flexibility in choosing search algorithms and search 
parameters.  It would be easiest if the function for numerical 
optimzation was written in S+ so that I could avoid (for now) 
learning to link  in C or fortran subroutines.

     Information about such functions or possible solutions based 
upon modifying nlmin or nlminb would be appreciated.  Thanks in 
advance.
