From michael.m.meyer at boeing.com  Tue Mar 30 17:15:16 1999
From: michael.m.meyer at boeing.com (Michael Meyer)
Date: Tue, 30 Mar 1999 09:15:16 -0800
Subject: [S] qqplot broken, qq ok.
Message-ID: <199903301715.JAA19457@desire.rt.cs.boeing.com>

The
        qqplot
function in Splus (3.4 and 5) has an unusual feature which I would call 
a bug.   Assume that you want to do a qqplot of two samples of 
different sizes.  qqplot will match the extremes of both samples and 
then interpolate in between.  This is not the right thing to do, and 
leads to very unusual plots.   For example, try
        qqplot(rnorm(10), rnorm(1000))
The max/min of rnorm(10) will be around 1.5 while the max/min of 
rnorm(1000) will be around 3.  Clearly, as the difference in sample 
sizes increases the ends of the plot become more skewed.

Fortunately the trellis  qq  function gets it right.

The quick and dirty fix to qqplot is to emulate the qq function, viz

"qqplot" <- 
  function(x, y, plot.it = TRUE, xlab = deparse(substitute(x)), ylab = deparse(
                                                                  
substitute(y)), ...)
{
  x <- sort(x)
  y <- sort(y)
  if(length(x) > length(y))
    x <- quantile(x,ppoints(length(y)))
  if(length(y) > length(x))
    y <- quantile(y,ppoints(length(x)))
  if(plot.it)
    plot(x, y, xlab = xlab, ylab = ylab, ...)
  invisible(list(x = x, y = y))
}

 --Mike

Mike Meyer, Applied Statistics, The Boeing Company

