From shannon at osler.wustl.edu  Wed Mar 31 15:59:07 1999
From: shannon at osler.wustl.edu (William Shannon)
Date: Wed, 31 Mar 1999 09:59:07 -0600
Subject: [S] Re: How to compare classifiers
Message-ID: <3702464B.9A1232CC@osler.wustl.edu>

Jean-Michel Gries wrote:

"I am trying to compare 4 classification method results to the true
assignments in order to rank them on the "quality" of assignment. Each
classification result is a categorical variable with 3 levels. For each
pseudo-observation I know the true assignment."

A classification results in an exhaustive partition of the objects into
non-overlapping subsets. Hubert and Arabie (Journal of Classification,
2:193-218, 1985) have a paper called 'Comparing Partitions' that uses
the 'corrected Rand statistic' to compare the performance of a
classifier with a gold standard (the true classification) or another
classifier.

This statistic operates as follows. Select two objects at random. Four
classification assignments can now occur:

        1. The classifier and gold standard assigns both objects to the same
class

        2. The classifier and gold standard assigns both objects to different
classes

        3. The classifier assigns both objects to the same class and the gold
standard assigns both objects to different classes

        4. The classifier assigns both objects to different classes and the
gold standard assigns both objects to the same class

1 and 2 indicate the classifier and gold standard are treating the pair
of objects identically, and 3 and 4 indicate the classifier and gold
standard are treating the pair of objects differently. Repeat this
classification assignment for all pairs of objects, a 'good' classifier,
when compared with a gold standard, results when classification
assignments 1 and 2 occurs most often.

This statistic is discussed in most recent cluster analysis textbooks.

Bill

